      "expected": "J4 pin 3 (SCK) should connect to the SCK net (ATtiny PB2), and J4 pin 4 (MOSI) should connect to the SDA net (ATtiny PB0).",
      "evidence": [
        {"source": "schematic", "quote": "J4 pin 3 SCK wire goes to global label 'SDA'"},
        {"source": "schematic", "quote": "J4 pin 4 MOSI wire goes to global label 'SCK'"}
      ],
      "impact": "ISP programming will fail because the programmer's SCK and MOSI signals are swapped, so the MCU cannot be programmed or configured.",
      "confidence": 0.95
    },
    {
      "category": "short_to_supply",
      "components": ["J6"],
      "pins": ["J6.1"],
      "nets": ["+5V", "SCK"],
      "observed": "J6 is labeled 'I2C' but its pin 1 is connected to +5V, and pin 2 is connected to the SCK net. A proper 2-pin I2C connector should carry SDA and SCK.",
      "expected": "J6, being an I2C connector, should have its two pins connected to the SDA and SCK nets (or at least not to a power rail).",
      "evidence": [
        {"source": "schematic", "quote": "J6 pin 1 wire connects to +5V power symbol"},
        {"source": "schematic", "quote": "J6 value is 'I2C'"}
      ],
      "impact": "If J6 is mated with another I2C device, the +5V rail will be shorted to the I2C bus, potentially damaging devices on the bus or the regulator.",
      "confidence": 0.85
    }
  ],
  "summary": "The schematic contains multiple serious errors: the ATtiny45/13 symbols have incorrect physical pin assignments (swapping reset and I/O pins), the ISP header has SCK and MOSI swapped, and the I2C connector J6 has +5V on a signal pin instead of an I2C line. These anomalies would prevent the board from being programmed and operating correctly."
}

